# Line-ending policy. Added 2026-07-30, on a narrower ground than the one that prompted it.
#
# WHAT THIS IS NOT FOR. It was written after `git add` warned that LF would become CRLF on
# `state/resource-ledger.jsonl` and `state/prompt-tickets.jsonl`, both hash-chained. The fear
# was that normalisation would break the chain and produce a false tamper report. THAT FEAR IS
# WRONG, and it was tested rather than assumed: `tools/bus/bus.py verify`, run against the
# eol-normalised index copy of `state/bus.jsonl` in an isolated tree, reports "chain intact
# across every chained row", identically to the disk copy. The chain hashes PARSED JSON
# content, not raw bytes, so line endings cannot reach it. An earlier hand-rolled check said
# otherwise and was wrong because it guessed the hash formula.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. Two real reasons, both about the Linux migration in
# docs/prd/2026-07-30-consolidation-and-migration.md:
#
# 1. A mixed-platform checkout of an append-only ledger produces whole-file diffs. If the same
#    repo is checked out on Windows (core.autocrlf true) and on WSL ext4, every ledger looks
#    entirely rewritten on each side, which destroys the property the ledgers exist for: that
#    an append is a one-line diff a human can read. `git show <rev>:state/x.jsonl` is the whole
#    of what this repo absorbed from Dolt (docs/analysis/2026-07-25-our-own-dolt.md section 3),
#    and it stops being readable when every line changes.
#
# 2. Shell scripts under dot-claude/hooks are executed by bash inside WSL after the migration.
#    A CRLF shebang line makes a script fail with a confusing "bad interpreter: no such file or
#    directory", naming an interpreter that plainly exists. That is the single most common
#    Windows-to-Linux migration failure and it is silent until something does not fire.

# Ledgers: byte-stable across platforms. -text disables all eol conversion in both directions.
state/*.jsonl   -text
state/*.log     -text
state/*.tsv     -text

# Shell scripts must reach a Linux interpreter with LF endings, or the shebang breaks.
*.sh            text eol=lf
dot-claude/hooks/*  text eol=lf

# PowerShell is Windows-side by nature and stays CRLF.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# Python: LF everywhere. Interpreters accept either, but a mixed tree produces noise diffs.
*.py            text eol=lf

# Generated artifacts and binaries: no conversion, no diff attempt.
*.exe           binary
*.sqlite        binary
*.db            binary
*.png           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.bundle        binary
docs/CODEBASE-MAP.md  -diff
